    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d800">

                <pb facs="0694" n="685" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg685"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/242: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-01-29T18:00:00+01:00">January 29, 1940—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 29—2:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>71. For Welles.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d800-f1"/> I learn from Kirk<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d800-f2"/> that Attolico, Italian Ambassador Berlin, has called upon
                    him to tell him of a recent conversation which the President had with
                        Colonna<anchor n="3" xml:id="d800-f3"/> during which the President, among
                    other things, emphasized in particular the change of attitude in the United
                    States towards Italy and the part which neutral countries might play in the
                    interests of peace. Attolico added that he assumed Ciano<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d800-f4"/> had discussed it with me. It seems clear, therefore, that
                    the Italian Government regards this conversation as of sufficient importance to
                    circularize it among its representatives in third countries.</p>
                <p>You will appreciate, I know, that it would have been helpful if I had been
                    advised of this conversation but perhaps it is not too late even now to
                    authorize me to refer to it sympathetically when I see Ciano.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d800-f1" n="1" xml:id="d800-f1-content">
                <p>Sumner Welles, Under Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d800-f2" n="2" xml:id="d800-f2-content">
                <p>Alexander C. Kirk, Chargé in Germany.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d800-f3" n="3" xml:id="d800-f3-content">
                <p>Ascanio dei principi Colonna, Italian Ambassador in the United States.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d800-f4" n="4" xml:id="d800-f4-content">
                <p>Galeazzo Ciano, Italian Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
